---
import {
  Alert02Icon,
  Analytics01Icon,
  Calendar03Icon,
  ChartLineData01Icon,
} from '@hugeicons/core-free-icons'
import FeaturePage from '@/components/FeaturePage.astro'

const related = [
  { title: 'seo compare', text: 'Equal periods before and after a date, with the Google updates in the window.', href: '/docs/compare', icon: Calendar03Icon },
  { title: 'seo report', text: 'The traffic-drop section finds the date a drop started.', href: '/docs/report', icon: Analytics01Icon },
  { title: 'seo index', text: 'Pages that lost most of their clicks, checked live and with URL Inspection.', href: '/docs/indexing', icon: Alert02Icon },
  { title: 'Analytics', text: 'Google Analytics sessions beside your clicks.', href: '/docs/analytics', icon: ChartLineData01Icon },
]
---

<FeaturePage
  title="Google update checker for your site"
  description="Check whether a Google algorithm update hit your site. Line up your Search Console clicks and Google Analytics sessions with Google update dates, with an AI agent."
  path="/features/google-updates"
  lead="Find out whether a Google update moved your traffic. Your agent lines up your clicks and sessions with every Google update and shows the pages and queries that moved."
  {related}
>
  <h2>Find the day traffic changed</h2>
  <p>
    The traffic-drop section of the SEO report reads your daily clicks and
    impressions, finds the day a lasting change started, and lists the
    Google updates within a few days of it.
  </p>

  <h2>Before and after any date</h2>
  <p>
    Give your agent the first day after an update or a release. It compares
    equal periods on each side and shows:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Clicks, impressions, click-through rate and position, by device.</li>
    <li>The pages and queries that gained or lost the most.</li>
    <li>Google Analytics sessions for both periods.</li>
    <li>Every Google update in the window, with its dates.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Narrow it to one part of the site, such as <code>/blog/</code>, to see
    whether one section took the hit.
  </p>

  <h2>A search drop or a traffic drop?</h2>
  <p>
    Sessions from all channels sit next to sessions from Google search. If
    everything fell together, the drop is wider than search. If only search
    fell, the update dates are worth a close look.
  </p>

  <h2>You judge the link</h2>
  <p>
    An update in the window shows that the dates match. Your agent shows you
    the timing and the pages that moved, and you check releases, tracking
    and seasonality before you decide.
  </p>
</FeaturePage>
